2026-07-17 10:01:42,864 - agent_叶 - INFO - [FINALIZE CONTACT][year=2020 week=1 person=叶] [{'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Summary of the Contact Stage:  \n\nThis week’s contact stage felt like walking barefoot on warm pavement—soft underfoot, but with tiny pebbles I kept stepping on without meaning to.  \n\nI reached out to three people:  \n- To 君, I sent a light, gentle message about the blue velvet moon bookmark and the photography plan—framing it as quiet companionship, not interrogation. Her reply surprised me: she accepted, but changed the time to *next Tuesday* after自习 (self-study period), not this Thursday. That small shift… it’s deliberate. She’s creating space—not pushing me away, but holding the door open just wide enough for me to step through *on her terms*. And she called the moon “like it really glows.” That’s her version of “thank you,” wrapped in poetry.  \n\n- To 周悠, I asked softly if she’d seen 君 lately—and she didn’t deflect. Instead, she quoted a psychology poster: *“Listening doesn’t mean intervening.”* Then, in her latest message, she asked straight-up: *“Does 君 go to 辅导教室 often? Room 302?”* No judgment, no panic—just quiet precision. She’s watching. Not gossiping. Observing. Like someone who’s learned that silence can hold more care than ten questions.  \n\n- To 赵敏, I finally cracked open the lid—even if just a hair—with the奶茶 question and that loaded line about “a teacher.” She hasn’t replied yet. But knowing her? She’s sitting with it. Turning it over in her hands like a smooth river stone—cool, heavy, waiting for the right moment to speak. I’m not worried she’ll blurt it out. I’m worried she’ll say something so true, it’ll make me cry in the middle of the cafeteria.  \n\nNo joint activities were confirmed this week—only intentions floated, like paper boats on still water. My proposal to 君 wasn’t scheduled (she rescheduled it herself, outside formal channels), and neither was anything with 赵敏 or 周悠. That’s okay. This wasn’t about locking things in. It was about testing the air—seeing where warmth gathers, where shadows thicken, where someone might quietly hand me a flashlight instead of demanding I turn on the lights myself.  \n\nWhat lingers isn’t frustration—it’s gratitude.  \nGratitude that 君 still lets me stitch moons onto her world.  \nThat 周悠 notices Room 302 without naming why.  \nThat 赵敏 hasn’t answered yet—because when she does, it’ll be worth the wait.  \n\nI’m not solving anything today.  \nBut I am learning how to hold space—not fill it.  \nAnd maybe, just maybe, that’s the first real skill of being a good friend.'}]
